    "message": "fix(routing): give a thinking model its own deadline, not the silent one's\n\n5.50.0 stopped reasoning from committing the route, which is right, but left\na held-reasoning attempt looking *silent* to routing -- so it inherited the\nfirst-token share, `FALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT` divided by the chain length. On\nthe reporter's eleven-model route that is 54 seconds, which is not enough\ntime for a model to think.\n\nMeasured against the same 161,812-row log that motivated 5.50.0:\n\n    successful requests that produced reasoning        105,078\n      slower than a 54.5s answer-deadline with\n      little or no answer -- i.e. diverted               1,387\n      same, against a 300s answer-deadline                  27\n    budget exhaustions that were reasoning-only             44\n\nSo as shipped it diverts roughly 1,387 successful requests to rescue 44\nfailures, and buys nothing for the tightness: every one of the 499 budget\nexhaustions ran the *full* 600s, so any allowance below that rescues all of\nthem. On a chain of similarly slow models those diversions can cascade into\noutright failures.\n\n`FALLBACK_REASONING_ANSWER_TIMEOUT` (default 300s) is a flat allowance\ninstead. The provider now emits an empty heartbeat while its holdback buffer\nis withholding thought frames; routing consumes it, keeps the attempt\nuncommitted, and starts the allowance from the first held thought. Measured\nfrom the first thought and not re-armed per thought on purpose -- a model\nlooping forever produces reasoning indefinitely, so a stall-style clock would\nnever fire on the exact failure this bounds.\n\nA stream that produces nothing at all sends no heartbeat and keeps the tight\nshare, so the fix that took hangs from 150 a day to roughly one is untouched.\n\nThe sentinel lives in `core/anthropic/stream_contracts.py`, not in the\nprovider package: routing may not import providers, and the contract test\nsaid so. Moved rather than shimmed.\n\nAlso corrects a number I published. 5.50.0 claimed \"479 of 499 budget\nexhaustions\" were this mechanism. 479 is how many ran on one *model*; I\ngrouped by `resolved_model` and read the top row as a mechanism count.\nGrouped by mechanism it is 44 -- 401 produced nothing at all and 54 produced\nsome answer. The mechanism and the reported failure are unchanged; only the\nprevalence was wrong, and it is corrected here, in the release notes and in\nthe PR.\n\nAnd documents what was never documented: `FALLBACK_ON_REASONING_ONLY` and\nthis setting are now in the README beside their siblings, along with\n`FALLBACK_STALL_TIMEOUT`, which had been missing since it shipped.\n\nVerification: 8 new tests. Fourteen mutations. Ten caught, four of them by\nhanging -- which for a deadline is the strongest result there is. Three more\nwere reported SKIPPED because `ruff format` had reflowed the lines my markers\nmatched, and were re-run against the formatted text and caught. One was a\nbroken probe that wrote a raw newline into a string literal and only proved\nthe file would not parse; the property it aimed at is unkillable by\nconstruction, so it is asserted directly instead. Full suite 5021 passed,\nwith the 8 pre-existing PowerShell installer failures unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
